ranker/git/commits{/sha}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/comments{/number}","issue_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/issues/comments{/number}","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/contents/{+path}","compare_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/compare/{base}...{head}","merges_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/merges","archive_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/{archive_format}{/ref}","downloads_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/downloads","issues_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/issues{/number}","pulls_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/pulls{/number}","milestones_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/milestones{/number}","notifications_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/notifications{?since,all,participating}","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/labels{/name}","releases_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/releases{/id}","deployments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/deployments","created_at":"2026-11-20T22:59:28Z","updated_at":"2026-11-21T05:16:45Z","pushed_at":"2026-11-21T06:05:38Z","git_url":"git://github.com/dbeallor/album-ranker.git","ssh_url":"git@github.com:dbeallor/album-ranker.git","clone_url":"https://github.com/dbeallor/album-ranker.git","svn_url":"https://github.com/dbeallor/album-ranker","homepage":null,"size":164,"stargazers_count":0,"watchers_count":0,"language":"TypeScript","has_issues":true,"has_projects":true,"has_downloads":false,"has_wiki":true,"has_pages":false,"has_discussions":false,"forks_count":0,"mirror_url":null,"archived":false,"disabled":false,"open_issues_count":11,"license":null,"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"has_pull_requests":true,"pull_request_creation_policy":"all","topics":[],"visibility":"public","forks":0,"open_issues":11,"watchers":0,"default_branch":"main","permissions":{"admin":true,"maintain":true,"push":true,"triage":true,"pull":true}},"body":"Put the app on AWS, in a staging and a production environment, so a phone and a laptop see the same rankings behind a password. The MVP runs on localhost only; this is the follow-up named in the MVP spec (#2).\n\n## Today\nNo code or deployment exists. The app is one Next.js process holding one SQLite file open through `node:sqlite` (`lib/db/server.ts`). The path comes from `DATABASE_PATH` in `lib/config.ts`, defaulting to `./data/album-ranker.db`, and the database migrates itself on first open. Config is read from environment variables. There is no Dockerfile, no hosting config and no password gate. `docs/agents/project.md` § Environments and deploy says \"none yet\".\n\n## Scope\n- The user creates the new AWS account. Creating it, billing, and any credentials or access keys are the user's job and are never committed to the repo. The README says what the user has to set up in the account first, and the deploy steps take credentials from the user's own machine or environment.\n- Staging and production both live in that one account. Each has its own running app, its own SQLite file on durable storage, and its own password, so staging never reads or writes production data.\n- Choose which AWS service runs the app and write down why. The app must run as exactly one copy with one writer to its SQLite file, and the file must survive restarts and redeploys, so a host with no persistent disk is ruled out unless the spec settles the cost tradeoff below. Say how the SQLite file is kept on durable storage and how it is backed up (backup is copying the file, which includes what is still in the WAL file). Include the rough monthly cost.\n- Cost constraint: as much of the AWS deployment as possible is pay-for-what-you-use (billed by requests, compute time or storage used), not long-running services billed while idle. One person uses this occasionally and does not want to pay constantly. This is a constraint on the design, not a choice of service; the spec decides the services. The spec lists each AWS component with how it is billed and an estimated monthly cost at idle for staging and production, and justifies any always-on, idle-billed component.\n- The one-copy, one-writer, durable-disk SQLite requirement above may conflict with compute that scales to zero. The spec settles that tradeoff and says what it costs (money, cold starts or anything else). This ticket does not resolve it.\n- Deploy the app and its SQLite file to both environments, with config from environment variables or AWS secret storage and no secrets in the repo.\n- Add a single shared password gate in front of the whole app: one user, no accounts. The password comes from config, and the app is not reachable without it. Production refuses to start without a password. The password gate is required in staging too, since both are reachable from the internet.\n- Serve both environments over HTTPS.\n- Write the deploy, promote-to-production and backup and restore steps in the README.\n- This exposes personal listening data beyond localhost, so the password gate is required before either environment is reachable from the internet.\n\n## Acceptance\n- Staging and production each ask for their own password and show nothing without it.\n- Both are served over HTTPS.\n- A change made on one device shows on another after reload, in each environment.\n- A change made in staging does not show in production.\n- The README steps deploy a fresh copy of each environment into an AWS account and restore from a copied database file.\n- The spec lists each AWS component with how it is billed and an estimated monthly cost at idle for staging and production, and justifies every always-on, idle-billed component.\n- The spec says how the one-copy, durable SQLite requirement is met alongside the cost constraint, and what that costs.\n- No AWS credential, secret, `.env` file or database file is committed.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Creating the AWS account or handling its credentials. The user does both.\n- Accounts, multiple users and sharing.\n- Any album, ranking or rating features.\n- Local preview environments (see #20) and browser test runs (see #19).\n- A custom domain and automatic deploys on merge, unless the user asks for them.\n\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/issues/14/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dbeallor/album-ranker/issues/14/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}},"event":"cross-referenced"}]